"""Evidence-driven sections for the explicitly requested four-hour extension."""
import json,statistics as st


def add_sections(root,rows,section,table,esc,lookup,fig):
    summary={}
    selection_path=root/'extended_long_selection.json'
    if selection_path.exists():
        choice=json.loads(selection_path.read_text())
        candidates={c['model']:c for c in choice['all_candidates']}
        selection_rows=[]
        for name in choice['selected']:
            c=candidates[name]
            selection_rows.append([esc(name),f"{c['selection_score']:.6f}",esc(choice['rationale'][name]['reason'])])
        section('Selection of the final long experiments','''<p>All four extended short screens completed before selecting these four KS2 families for 100,000 updates on both training pools, with seeds 11, 22 and 33: 24 runs. The unchanged original MLP 100k checkpoints remain the reference. Selection uses complete three-seed exploratory results and distinct mechanisms, with no reserved scores.</p><p>Rank-1 and rank-4 learned spines each have eager and fused implementation repeats. Their selection scores average both implementations within each seed before averaging the three seeds. These are three independent seeds, not six. The fused implementation is used for speed; its more favorable trajectory is not selected as a separate architecture.</p>'''+table(['Selected family','Short-run selection loss','Reason and limits'],selection_rows)+'''<p>Frozen full geometry has 603,648 learned parameters plus 150,880 fixed geometry values: 754,528 coefficients in total. Its learned parameter count is below the MLP's 615,936, but its total coefficient storage is higher. A quality lead for this candidate would therefore not establish a matched-total-storage advantage. Frozen spine variants carry much less fixed geometry.</p><p>The frozen-bias screen had a strong mean but substantial seed spread. Frozen gain and rank 2 also improved the short mean; they were deferred to retain rank, trainability and basis-conditioning tests within the remaining time. Their omission does not establish that they would lose at 100k. <a href="extended_long_selection.json">Complete selection record and candidates</a> · <a href="extended_selection_tradeoffs.json">Selection tradeoffs</a> · <a href="wave13_selected_long_manifest.json">Exact 24-run manifest</a>.</p>''')
        summary['selected_long_families']=choice['selected']
    longrows=[]
    extended_long=[r for r in rows if r['steps']>=100000 and r['wave']!='wave8_long' and r['family']=='dense_ks2']
    for pool in ('original','larger'):
        poolrows=[r for r in extended_long if ('larger' if r['data_train_bytes']>10000000 else 'original')==pool]
        for name in sorted({r['config']['name'] for r in poolrows}):
            rs=[r for r in poolrows if r['config']['name']==name]
            if not rs:continue
            paired=[(r,lookup('wave8_long',f'{pool}_original_mlp_{r["seed"]}')) for r in rs]
            paired=[(r,m) for r,m in paired if m]
            values=[r['full_validation_loss'] for r,m in paired];gaps=[r['full_validation_loss']-m['full_validation_loss'] for r,m in paired]
            longrows.append(dict(pool=pool,model=name,n=len(values),mean=st.mean(values),sd=st.stdev(values) if len(values)>1 else None,mean_paired_gap=st.mean(gaps),wins=sum(g<0 for g in gaps),seeds={r['seed']:r['full_validation_loss'] for r,m in paired}))
    section('Extended long-run geometry tests','''<p>The user extended exploration through 07:22:13 UTC. These tests reuse the exact original MLP 100k checkpoints as the reference. Warp-only, slow geometry learning, and frozen geometry are KS2 interventions; no MLP setting is changed. The missing frozen-geometry comparison on the larger pool is included. All runs use the same fresh 100k cosine schedule and the same three seeds as the primary reference.</p>'''+fig('extended_long_gaps','Individual paired-seed gaps against the unchanged original MLP 100k reference.')+table(['Training pool','KS2 model','Seeds complete','Mean loss','Seed SD','KS2 − original MLP','Seed wins'],[[x['pool'],x['model'],x['n'],f'{x["mean"]:.6f}',f'{x["sd"]:.6f}' if x['sd'] is not None else '—',f'{x["mean_paired_gap"]:+.6f}',f'{x["wins"]}/{x["n"]}'] for x in longrows])+'''<p>Partial cohorts remain provisional. A small mean difference with mixed seed signs is treated as a practical tie rather than a dependable improvement.</p>''')
    summary['long_groups']=longrows
    training_gaps=root/'selected_long_training_gaps.json'
    if training_gaps.exists():
        fit=json.loads(training_gaps.read_text())['rows']
        section('Training fit and validation improvement',table(['Pool','100k KS2 variant','Sampled train gap vs MLP','Full validation gap vs MLP'],[[r['pool'],esc(r['model']),f"{r['mean_train_gap']:+.6f}",f"{r['mean_full_val_gap']:+.6f}"] for r in fit])+'''<p>On the larger pool, rank-1 KS2 improves both the recorded training-sample loss (about −0.00513) and exploratory validation loss (about −0.00499) relative to the unchanged MLP. Its observed benefit is therefore not solely a case of worse training fit accompanied by better validation. On the original pool, its training-sample gap is only −0.00052 and its validation gap is +0.00107; these small differences do not identify a precise overfitting mechanism.</p><p>The sampled training evaluation uses the same 65,536 target positions within each data pool and pairs the same seeds. Training and validation text have different difficulty, so absolute train-minus-validation losses are not interpreted as a direct generalization measure. Changing pool size also changes text diversity and repetition frequency. <a href="selected_long_training_gaps.json">Individual paired-seed differences</a>.</p>''')
    if (root/'extended_long_trajectories.json').exists():
        section('Extended learning curves at matching horizons',fig('selected_long_trajectories','Paired-seed loss gaps at 15k, 50k and 100k updates within the same 100k learning-rate schedule.')+'''<p>Each checkpoint is compared to the unchanged MLP at the same seed, data pool and number of updates within its 100k schedule. This distinguishes early optimization gains from gains that survive longer training. Shading shows the range across seeds, not a statistical confidence interval. These 15k checkpoints must not be equated with the separately annealed 15k screens, whose learning-rate trajectory differs.</p><p><a href="extended_long_trajectories.json">All individual checkpoint losses and paired gaps</a>.</p>''')
    shortrefs={11:lookup('initial','mlp',11),22:lookup('initial','mlp',22),33:lookup('wave3','mlp_seed33',33)}
    for wave,title,intro in [
        ('wave14_spine_scale','Scaling the spine input without scaling the bypass','''<p>A final adaptive follow-up tests the rank-input statistics directly. Rank-8 geometry inputs are multiplied by √8, while rank-1 inputs are multiplied by 1/√8. A third configuration combines rank-8 scaling with the power basis. Only the input to spine geometry changes: learned projection tensors, raw coordinates in the bypass, output activation and parameter count are preserved. These are function-changing interventions, unlike the initial-function-preserving basis conversion.</p><p>All three seeds are specified for each configuration, at 15k updates on the original pool. The original MLP is the primary reference. The corresponding eager rank-8, rank-1 and power-basis cohorts above provide within-KS2 comparisons. CPU checks verify all nine initialized states, unchanged original MLP and optimizer settings, nonzero-readout forward values and all gradients. Per-GPU validation precedes training. Runs use cards released by the 100k queue and remain subject to the exploration cutoff. <a href="spine_scale_followup_plan.json">Recorded motivation and plan</a> · <a href="spine_scale_cpu_check.json">CPU verification</a>.</p>'''),
        ('wave11_conditioning','KS2 basis conditioning and chart rank','''<p>The power and Taylor bases are invertible reparameterizations of the original four Hermite terms. Coefficients are transformed so the initial mathematical function is preserved; the optimizer now acts in different coordinates. The two warped Chebyshev terms remain. This tests optimization conditioning without claiming a larger function class. Independent CPU checks compare geometry values, coordinate gradients, and complete nonzero-readout model logits.</p><p>Rank 1, 2, 4, 8 and 16 spine variants retain 368 projected features and identical input projection tensors. Regrouping changes the shared scalar mean supplied to each spine and slightly changes warp parameter count. Rank 1 tests whether cross-coordinate coupling itself helps; it still retains KS2 geometry. In the spine-only cell, every output within a chart evaluates its basis at the same scalar mean of that chart: G_j(c) = sum_m a_mj phi_m(mean(c)). At 368 projected features, rank 8 supplies 46 distinct scalar means, whereas rank 1 supplies 368. The 6 coefficients per projected feature remain; rank 1 adds per-chart warp parameters. Thus this comparison changes the diversity of the geometry inputs as well as coordinate grouping. The coordinate ReLU² path still has 368 features in either case, and each learned input projection can mix all 96 model dimensions. A rank-1 advantage would support independent learned scalar geometry in this harness, not establish that richer cross-coordinate geometry never helps. Full-geometry rank 2 and 4 variants instead reallocate the total parameter budget between feature width and quadratic-cost edges. Geometry-only RMS normalization preserves the raw coordinate bypass. Parallel and after-activation placements test whether applying ReLU² after geometry is the limiting interaction. Every model stays within the original 615,936 parameter budget.</p>'''),
        ('wave12_fixed_geometry','How much learned geometry adaptation is needed?','''<p>Full frozen geometry and spine frozen geometry are compared at the same 368 projected features. The earlier 31-chart frozen-full model had a smaller projected feature width and did not isolate this question. Frozen coefficients count as storage and computation, but not learned parameters. Full frozen geometry at 46 charts adds about 0.60 MB of fixed coefficients, whereas frozen spine geometry adds about 0.037 MB; both use the same learned projection width. Further interventions learn only a per-feature geometry bias, gain, both, or warps plus bias. Bias initializes to zero and gain to one, preserving the initial fixed-geometry function. Shared attention and original MLP remain unchanged.</p>'''),
        ('wave12b_rank_freezing','Interaction between chart rank and freezing','''<p>This follow-up compares learned and frozen spine geometry at ranks 1 and 4, using identical 368-feature projection tensors and the same fused implementation. It tests whether the freezing result depends on cross-coordinate grouping. A full rank-1 KS2 model uses 344 scalar charts and 615,488 learned parameters, testing richer scalar nonlinear geometry within the unchanged MLP budget. The new kernels must pass component, trained-model and captured-training checks before dispatch. Rank-1 spine models retain 368 features; the full rank-1 model has fewer features because its edge terms use part of the budget.</p>'''),
        ('wave12c_original_geometry','Original KS2 equation and bounded geometry inputs','''<p>Source review of RHAC identified a specific cell difference. Its local geometry uses the original mean and coordinate-sum weight decomposition, with softplus strengths multiplying absolute deviation and squared coordinate weights. The stabilized cell in this harness caps the spine, uses energy-normalized damping predicates, and bounds damping strengths. RHAC also feeds its geometry sigmoid(g/1.5). The following tests restore the original equation, apply sigmoid only to geometry inputs, and combine both. A fourth case adds the original positive-input geometry after the coordinate ReLU². Raw coordinate residuals and all projection tensors remain; each configuration has 613,168 learned parameters.</p><p>This is an adaptive follow-up motivated by source review, not a full RHAC reproduction. The primary MLP is unchanged. The local original equation was checked against the archived RHAC implementation for values and all gradients at positive, signed and zero-sum inputs. The four model factories preserve the original initialized tensors and zero-output function, pass nonzero-readout gradient and causality checks, and use eager geometry implementations. <a href="original_geometry_cpu_check.json">CPU checks</a>.</p>''')]:
        found=[r for r in rows if r['wave']==wave];groups=[]
        for name in sorted({r['config']['name'] for r in found}):
            rs=[r for r in found if r['config']['name']==name];vals=[r['full_validation_loss'] for r in rs];gaps=[r['full_validation_loss']-shortrefs[r['seed']]['full_validation_loss'] for r in rs]
            cfg=rs[0]['config'];groups.append(dict(model=name,n=len(rs),mean=st.mean(vals),sd=st.stdev(vals) if len(vals)>1 else None,gap=st.mean(gaps),parameters=rs[0]['parameters'],buffer_bytes=rs[0].get('buffer_bytes'),rank=cfg.get('subspace_rank',8),charts=cfg['charts'],seeds={r['seed']:r['full_validation_loss'] for r in rs},ms=st.median(r['ms_per_step'] for r in rs)))
        section(title,intro+fig(wave,'Individual exploratory validation scores; all available seeds and fixed reference means.')+table(['Model','Rank × charts','Learned parameters','Fixed buffers KiB','Seeds','Mean loss','Seed SD','Gap vs fixed MLP','Observed ms/step'],[[x['model'],f'{x["rank"]} × {x["charts"]}',f'{x["parameters"]:,}',f'{x["buffer_bytes"]/1024:.1f}' if x['buffer_bytes'] is not None else '—',x['n'],f'{x["mean"]:.6f}',f'{x["sd"]:.6f}' if x['sd'] is not None else '—',f'{x["gap"]:+.6f}',f'{x["ms"]:.2f}'] for x in groups])+'''<p>The reference is the unchanged original MLP 15k cohort, mean 1.620779. These are adaptive exploratory comparisons; all seeds are shown in the result ledger. Observed training times span different cards and clocks, so controlled timing is reported separately.</p>''')
        summary[wave]=groups
    section('Why chart rank changes the spine pathway','''<p>The spine-only geometry computes one scalar summary per chart: <code>t = 4 tanh(mean(c) / 4)</code>, then each chart output is <code>G_j(c) = Σ_m a_mj φ_m(t)</code>. The six basis responses depend on the same scalar t. Away from nondifferentiable boundaries, the geometry Jacobian has the outer-product form <code>J_G = (dG/dt) (dt/dc)</code>, so it has rank at most one within each chart. This is an algebraic property of the implemented spine, not an empirical estimate of the whole model's information capacity.</p><p>At 368 projected features, rank-8 charts supply 46 independently evaluated scalar summaries; rank-4 charts supply 92; rank-1 charts supply 368. Each output retains its own six coefficients, but smaller charts give those coefficients more distinct scalar inputs. That is a plausible explanation for the replicated small-chart advantage. The experiment also changes the number of per-chart warp parameters, so it does not isolate scalar-summary count perfectly.</p><p>The decoder itself is not restricted to a rank-one Jacobian per chart: it carries the coordinate bypass <code>c + G(c)</code>, followed by ReLU² and a learned output projection. Each learned input coordinate can mix all 96 model dimensions before chart grouping. Therefore, a rank-1 chart is a learned nonlinear function of a full-model projection, not a decision to ignore the other 95 model dimensions. Full geometry adds coordinate-dependent edges and damping, which do not obey the spine-only rank bound.</p><p>This separates two hypotheses. More independent scalar responses may be a better use of the small decoder budget; alternatively, smaller charts may be easier to optimize. The learned/frozen rank comparisons and the function-preserving basis experiments help distinguish these explanations, while the 100k runs test whether the short-horizon ordering persists.</p>''')
    holdout_path=root/'final_checks/holdout_results.json'
    rank_probe=root/'rank_input_statistics.json'
    if rank_probe.exists():
        evidence=json.loads(rank_probe.read_text());rank_rows=[];rank_summary=[]
        for name in dict.fromkeys(r['model'] for r in evidence['rows']):
            for state in ('initialized','trained_15k'):
                cells=[layer for r in evidence['rows'] if r['model']==name and r['state']==state for layer in r['layers']]
                if not cells:continue
                item=dict(model=name,state=state,layer_seed_observations=len(cells),coordinate_rms=st.mean(x['coordinate_rms'] for x in cells),basis_argument_rms=st.mean(x['basis_argument_rms'] for x in cells),condition=st.median(x['basis_design_condition_median'] for x in cells),participation=st.mean(x['summary_covariance_participation_ratio'] for x in cells),active=st.mean(x['relu_active_fraction'] for x in cells))
                rank_summary.append(item)
                rank_rows.append([esc(name),state,item['layer_seed_observations'],f"{item['coordinate_rms']:.3f}",f"{item['basis_argument_rms']:.3f}",f"{item['condition']:.0f}",f"{item['participation']:.1f}",f"{item['active']:.3f}"])
        summary['rank_input_statistics']=rank_summary
        section('Chart rank also changes basis scale and conditioning','''<p>On the same 1,024 training targets, all five learned-rank variants start with the same projected-coordinate RMS, about 0.513. Averaging within charts reduces the scalar supplied to the basis: its RMS is 0.505 at rank 1, 0.359 at rank 2, 0.256 at rank 4, 0.181 at rank 8 and 0.126 at rank 16. Thus chart grouping changes both the number of distinct scalar summaries and their initial scale.</p><p>The sampled basis-design condition number is about 103 at rank 1 versus 7,900 at rank 8 at initialization. At 15k updates, basis-input RMS is much closer (0.796 versus 0.771), but the corresponding condition summaries remain about 128 versus 2,024. Learned projections partly compensate for averaging, while the basis correlations and distribution of responses still differ. This supports conditioning as one plausible mechanism; it is not a causal decomposition of the observed loss advantage, and a design-matrix condition number is not the optimizer Hessian.</p>'''+fig('rank_input_statistics','Training-input statistics across chart rank, before and after 15k updates.')+table(['Model','State','Layer × seed observations','Coordinate RMS','Basis-input RMS','Median basis condition','Summary covariance participation','ReLU² active fraction'],rank_rows)+'''<p>RMS values, participation ratios and active fractions are averaged across four layers and three seeds. Condition values are medians of the 12 per-layer/seed median chart condition numbers. The covariance participation ratio is variance weighted effective dimension, not algebraic rank; it depends on sampled activations and can change under unequal feature rescaling. Trained models are measured at their own learned activations. Frozen models retain input dependence through learned projections, and their different statistics do not establish a counterfactual training outcome.</p><p>The probe uses CPU eager inference, identical training windows and no validation or reserved targets. <a href="rank_input_statistics.json">All individual layer/seed statistics and sampled positions</a>.</p>''')
    if holdout_path.exists():
        holdout=json.loads(holdout_path.read_text());by_run={r['source'].removesuffix('/completed.json'):r for r in rows}
        measured={(r.get('phase','long'),r['job']):r['evaluation']['loss'] for r in holdout['rows']}
        grouped={}
        for h in holdout['rows']:
            if not h.get('phase','').startswith('extended_'):continue
            r=by_run[h['run']];pool='larger' if r['data_train_bytes']>10000000 else 'original';seed=r['seed']
            key=(r['wave'],pool,r['steps'],r['config']['name'])
            reference_key=('long',f'{pool}_original_mlp_{seed}') if r['steps']==100000 else ('short',f'original_mlp_{seed}')
            assert reference_key in measured,reference_key
            grouped.setdefault(key,[]).append(dict(seed=seed,loss=h['evaluation']['loss'],reference=measured[reference_key],exploration=r['full_validation_loss'],run=h['run']))
        held_groups=[]
        for (wave,pool,steps,name),values in sorted(grouped.items()):
            gaps=[r['loss']-r['reference'] for r in values]
            held_groups.append(dict(wave=wave,pool=pool,steps=steps,model=name,n=len(values),mean=st.mean(r['loss'] for r in values),reference_mean=st.mean(r['reference'] for r in values),mean_gap=st.mean(gaps),gap_sd=st.stdev(gaps) if len(gaps)>1 else None,wins=sum(v<0 for v in gaps),individual=values))
        summary['reserved_groups']=held_groups
        section('Extended study on the reserved validation interval','''<p>All predeclared extended candidates are scored on the previously untouched validation interval after exploration ends. No architecture, optimizer or training selection uses these scores. Short and long runs are compared only to the original unchanged MLP at the same horizon and training pool. Absolute loss differences between shards reflect text difficulty; the paired within-shard gap is the relevant comparison.</p>'''+table(['Wave','Pool','Steps','KS2 model','Paired seeds','KS2 mean','Fixed MLP mean','KS2 − MLP','Gap seed SD','KS2 seed wins'],[[x['wave'],x['pool'],f"{x['steps']:,}",x['model'],x['n'],f"{x['mean']:.6f}",f"{x['reference_mean']:.6f}",f"{x['mean_gap']:+.6f}",f"{x['gap_sd']:.6f}" if x['gap_sd'] is not None else '—',f"{x['wins']}/{x['n']}"] for x in held_groups])+'''<p>These are three-seed replications on one additional text interval, not evidence of an advantage across all LLM scales or corpora. The raw artifact retains every candidate and individual seed, including unfavorable comparisons.</p>''')
    path=root/'geometry_counterfactual.json'
    if path.exists():
        probe=json.loads(path.read_text());probe_rows=[]
        for r in probe['rows']:
            effects={x['mode']:x['delta'] for x in r['ablations'] if x['layer']==-1}
            probe_rows.append([esc(r['job']),f'{effects["zero"]:+.4f}',f'{effects["mean_only"]:+.4f}',f'{effects["centered_only"]:+.4f}',', '.join(f'{x["mean_fraction_squared_norm"]:.2f}' for x in r['layers']),', '.join(f'{x["basis_design_condition_median"]:.0f}' for x in r['layers'])])
        section('Geometry offsets, input dependence and basis conditioning','''<p>On 2,048 fixed exploratory validation targets, inference perturbations remove geometry, replace it with its training-calibrated mean, or subtract that mean. Constants are calibrated on a separate 2,048 training targets. No weights are retrained and no reserved validation is used. Positive deltas mean worse loss. These are dependence tests, not estimates of what a retrained simpler model would achieve.</p>'''+table(['Trained checkpoint','Remove all geometry','Keep only mean','Remove only mean','Mean share of geometry squared norm, layers 0–3','Median basis design condition number, layers 0–3'],probe_rows)+'''<p>Both the learned offset and input-dependent part matter. Frozen geometry also remains input dependent because learned projections choose where its fixed curves are evaluated. The large condition numbers identify correlated basis directions on sampled activations; they motivate reparameterization tests but do not prove ill-conditioning caused the loss gap. The mean-energy share and basis statistics use training inputs. Conditioning estimates are descriptive and checkpoint dependent.</p><p><a href="geometry_counterfactual.json">Full perturbation results, including per-layer changes and half/1.5× geometry scaling</a>.</p>''')
    terms_path=root/'replicated_geometry_terms.json'
    if terms_path.exists():
        probes=json.loads(terms_path.read_text())['rows'];terms_rows=[]
        for layer in range(4):
            ratio=st.mean(r['layers'][layer]['edge_rms']/r['layers'][layer]['spine_rms'] for r in probes)
            edge=st.mean(r['inference_ablations'][f'layer{layer}_spine_only']-r['base_loss'] for r in probes)
            spine=st.mean(r['inference_ablations'][f'layer{layer}_edge_only']-r['base_loss'] for r in probes)
            terms_rows.append([layer,f'{ratio:.3f}',f'{edge:+.5f}',f'{spine:+.5f}'])
        section('Replicated dependence on spine and edge terms','''<p>Across three independently trained full-geometry KS2 models, edge and spine outputs have comparable RMS amplitude. Removing either contribution from any individual layer increases loss on the same 4,096 exploratory validation targets. The edge geometry is therefore used by these trained models. Its parameter cost may still be less effective than allocating more projected features; inference dependence and the best retrained parameter allocation are different questions.</p>'''+table(['Layer','Mean edge/spine RMS ratio','Mean loss delta after removing edges','Mean loss delta after removing spine'],terms_rows)+'''<p><a href="replicated_geometry_terms.json">All three seeds and per-layer interventions</a>. These measurements do not justify pruning the trained edge terms without retraining.</p>''')
    basis_path=root/'basis_condition_probe.json'
    if basis_path.exists():
        evidence=json.loads(basis_path.read_text())['rows'];basis_rows=[]
        for job in dict.fromkeys(r['job'] for r in evidence):
            for layer in range(4):
                group={r['basis']:r for r in evidence if r['job']==job and r['layer']==layer}
                basis_rows.append([esc(job),layer]+[f"{group[k]['condition_median']:.0f}" for k in ('hermite','powers','taylor')])
        section('Equivalent-basis conditioning probe','''<p>Design-matrix singular values were measured on 1,024 fixed training targets, using exactly the same sampled coordinates and warps for all bases. The first four Hermite terms span the same cubic polynomial space as powers or Taylor-scaled powers; coefficients are transformed before training so the initial function is unchanged. Power coordinates reduce the observed design condition number substantially in these checkpoints. This is a property of the sampled basis, not a measurement of the full optimizer Hessian and not proof of better generalization.</p>'''+table(['Checkpoint','Layer','Hermite condition','Power condition','Taylor condition'],basis_rows)+'''<p><a href="basis_condition_probe.json">Full design-SVD results</a> also include column-RMS-balanced condition numbers and feature scales. The training comparisons above decide whether the numerical difference is useful.</p>''')
    path=root/'structured_model_cpu_real_check.json'
    if path.exists():
        r=json.loads(path.read_text());gpu=root/'compact_code_checks/trained_model_real.json';speed=root/'compact_code_checks/speed_results.json'
        text=f'''<p>The original fixed codes can be represented by per-chart permutations, inverse permutations and post-Hadamard signs. Indices fit in signed 16-bit storage; signs use 8 bits. Identical code tables are shared across decoder layers and projections. FP32 blockwise Walsh transforms and folding implement the same linear map, with a different floating-point reduction order. All learned parameters remain unchanged.</p><p>For the trained 100k demuxed-history model, fixed-buffer storage drops from {r['original_buffer_bytes']:,} to {r['compact_buffer_bytes']:,} bytes. CPU validation logits differ by at most {r['logit_max_error']:.3g}; loss differs by {abs(r['original_loss']-r['compact_loss']):.3g}. Individual projection and transpose derivatives are independently checked across 96/2144/2336/2528/2720 input widths.</p>'''
        if gpu.exists():
            g=json.loads(gpu.read_text());text+=f'<p>Trained GPU check: {g["status"]}, max logit error {g["logit_max_error"]:.3g}. All parameter-gradient relative errors are retained in the linked artifact.</p>'
        else:text+='<p>Trained GPU equivalence and timing are pending; reduced storage alone does not establish faster training.</p>'
        if speed.exists():
            results=json.loads(speed.read_text());text+=table(['Projection implementation','Median ms/step','Five randomized rounds'],[[x['name'],f'{x["median_ms"]:.3f}',', '.join(f'{v:.3f}' for v in x['values'])] for x in results['summary']])
            measured={x['name']:x['median_ms'] for x in results['summary']}
            ratio=measured['history_compact']/measured['history_dense']
            text+=f'<p>The compact implementation takes {ratio:.2f}× the dense-code step time in this B4 × T64 FP32 test. Its smaller representation therefore does not deliver a training-speed improvement here. The long quality experiments retain the validated dense projections; this result applies to the tested implementation and workload, not to every possible implicit Hadamard kernel.</p>'
        isolated=root/'compact_code_checks/isolated_projection_speed.json'
        if isolated.exists():
            measures=json.loads(isolated.read_text())['summary'];by_case={(x['width'],x['operator']):x['median_ms'] for x in measures}
            timing_rows=[]
            for width in sorted({x['width'] for x in measures}):
                for direction in ('mux','transpose'):
                    dense=by_case[(width,'dense_'+direction)];compact=by_case[(width,'compact_'+direction)]
                    timing_rows.append([width,direction,f'{dense:.4f}',f'{compact:.4f}',f'{compact/dense:.2f}×'])
            text+='<h3>Which fixed operator costs time?</h3>'+table(['Input code width','Direction','Dense ms','Compact ms','Compact/dense'],timing_rows)+'<p>These isolated FP32 operators use batch 256, three randomized rounds and captured-kernel timing. The transpose is the backward linear map. They diagnose where time is spent; full-step measurements above determine the practical speed.</p>'
        text+='''<p>The initial random-token stress check flagged two near-zero gradient components under an elementwise tolerance. Reporting was changed to per-parameter relative L2 with all absolute errors preserved; the worst CPU relative L2 was 4.91e−6. This is numerical agreement, not bitwise identity. The real-input check uses exploratory validation bytes. <a href="structured_validation_notes.json">Validation notes</a> · <a href="structured_codes_cpu_check.json">Projection checks</a> · <a href="structured_model_cpu_real_check.json">Trained CPU check</a>.</p>'''
        section('Compact implementation of the original Hadamard codes',text)
    general=root/'compact_code_checks/general_geometry_check.json'
    if not general.exists() and (root/'compact_code_checks/general_geometry_check_first_failure.log').exists():
        section('Generalized geometry kernels and frozen-parameter savings','''<p>The first GPU validation attempt found a rank-4 input-gradient component outside the strict elementwise tolerance. The generalized backend has not been released to training. Diagnostic runs preserve failing tensors for comparison with an independent float64 autograd reference. Existing validated full and spine kernels remain in use.</p><p><a href="compact_code_checks/general_geometry_check_first_failure.log">First numerical-check failure</a>. Investigation and repeated GPU validation are pending.</p>''')
    if general.exists():
        check=json.loads(general.read_text())
        text=f'''<p>Equivalent fused geometry kernels cover ranks 1, 2, 4, 8 and 16 in Hermite, power and Taylor coordinates. The GPU checker passed {check['component_cases']} forward/gradient cases, including extreme coordinates and fully learned, warp-only and frozen geometry. Trained model comparisons and captured optimizer updates also passed. The artifact records the exact kernel source hash used to gate subsequent training.</p><p>Frozen geometry still needs derivatives with respect to its inputs so the learned projections can train. The optimized backward pass keeps these derivatives while omitting unused geometry-parameter gradients. Warp-only training computes the warp derivatives but skips coefficient and damping-parameter reductions. These are implementation changes, not changes to the model function or learned parameter budget.</p>'''
        if check.get('stress_roundoff'):
            text+='''<p>The initial strict elementwise check flagged three adversarial cases: two input gradients near zero coordinates and one forward result after large mixed-sign coordinates were clamped. Saved tensors were compared with independent CPU float64 autograd. Tiny-input gradient relative L2 errors were about 2×10⁻⁷ for the fused implementation; the clamp-stress forward error was 1.97×10⁻⁵. The eager reference also showed rounding error. Kernel code was unchanged. Ordinary-input and parameter-gradient tolerances remain strict; only these stress categories can use float64 checks, with relative L2 bounds of 10⁻⁵ for tiny input gradients and 5×10⁻⁵ for clamp forward outputs. Trained-model tolerances were unchanged.</p><p><a href="general_kernel_diagnostic/components.json">All 450 diagnostic cases and strict failures</a> · <a href="general_kernel_diagnostic/fp64_comparison.json">Independent float64 comparisons</a> · <a href="compact_code_checks/general_geometry_check_first_failure.log">Original failure log</a>.</p>'''
        speeds=root/'compact_code_checks/general_geometry_speed.json'
        if speeds.exists():
            records=json.loads(speeds.read_text())['summary'];timing_rows=[]
            for name in dict.fromkeys(x['model'] for x in records):
                values={x['backend']:x['median_ms'] for x in records if x['model']==name}
                target=values.get('skip_unused_gradients',values.get('fused'))
                timing_rows.append([esc(name),f"{values['eager']:.4f}",f"{values['previous_fused']:.4f}" if 'previous_fused' in values else '—',f'{target:.4f}',f"{values['eager']/target:.2f}×"])
            text+=table(['Geometry case','Eager ms','Previous fused ms','Selected fused ms','Eager/fused'],timing_rows)
            text+='<p>Isolated forward and required backward derivatives only: 256 input tokens, three randomized captured-kernel timing rounds on GPU0. These ratios exclude attention, projections and the optimizer; whole-step timings must be used for practical comparisons.</p>'
        text+='<p><a href="compact_code_checks/general_geometry_check.json">Numerical and trained-model GPU checks</a> · <a href="general_geometry_compile_check.json">Offline MI100 compilation checks</a>.</p>'
        section('Generalized geometry kernels and frozen-parameter savings',text)
        summary['general_geometry_validation']=dict(status=check['status'],component_cases=check['component_cases'],trained_cases=len(check['trained_models']),source_sha256=check['source_sha256'])
    components=root/'compact_code_checks/component_speed.json'
    if components.exists():
        probe=json.loads(components.read_text());component_rows=[]
        for model in probe['models']:
            values={component:sum(x['median_ms'] for x in probe['summary'] if x['model']==model['model'] and x['component']==component) for component in ('attention','ffn','down','geometry','up')}
            component_rows.append([esc(model['model'])]+[f'{values[k]:.3f}' if k!='geometry' or values[k] else '—' for k in ('attention','ffn','down','geometry','up')])
        section('Where the compute is spent inside trained blocks','''<p>Each isolated component is evaluated at real training-input activations from its trained checkpoint. Timings include forward and input/learned-parameter derivatives, use batch 4 × length 64 in FP32, and take the median of three randomized GPU0 rounds. The table sums these medians across the four layers. The original MLP is measured without changing its weights or architecture.</p>'''+table(['Trained model','RWKV ms','Complete FFN ms','FFN input projection ms','KS2 geometry ms','FFN output projection ms'],component_rows)+'''<p>The complete FFN includes its projections and geometry/activation: its subcolumns must not be added to it. Isolated kernels do not reproduce all whole-model scheduling and memory interactions, and these are not percentages of total training time. Norms, time mixing outside attention, residual additions, embedding/head, loss, gradient clipping and optimizer updates are excluded. Whole-step benchmarks remain the practical speed measurement.</p><p><a href="compact_code_checks/component_speed.json">Per-layer timings and exact measured configurations</a>.</p>''')
        attention=sum(x['median_ms'] for x in probe['summary'] if x['model']=='frozen_spine' and x['component']=='attention')
        decoder=sum(x['median_ms'] for x in probe['summary'] if x['model']=='frozen_spine' and x['component']=='ffn')
        section('Compute limits after simplifying KS2',f'''<p>For the frozen-spine model, the four isolated RWKV forward/backward modules total {attention:.3f} ms, compared with {decoder:.3f} ms for its four complete FFNs. This shows a substantial common compute cost once the decoder is cheap. It does not establish that attention capacity causes the loss gap. Further KS2 kernel savings can have limited whole-step impact when attention, optimizer and other operations remain unchanged.</p>''')
        summary['component_timing']=probe['summary']
    recovery=root/'evaluation_recovery_notes.json'
    if recovery.exists():
        recovered=json.loads(recovery.read_text())
        section('Numerical sensitivity of the original signed-coordinate equation','''<p>The original unbounded-coordinate KS2 equation amplified FP32 batch-size differences in evaluation. One first-seed validation window differed by about 0.0004 nats/byte between batches of 64 and 4, exceeding the evaluator's strict invariance tolerance. All three signed-original-equation models are therefore scored at their training batch size of 4 on exactly the same validation windows and targets. Other model families retain the original evaluation batch and strict check. Training completed normally; no weights, updates or optimizer states were changed or restarted. This numerical sensitivity is itself a limitation of that variant.</p>'''+table(['Run','Batch-4 full-shard loss','Largest batch-size probe difference','Offsets exceeding strict tolerance'],[[x['job'],f"{x['batch4_loss']:.6f}",f"{x['max_probe_batch_error']:.6g}",x['exceptions']] for x in recovered['rows']])+'''<p><a href="evaluation_recovery_notes.json">Recovery record, unchanged checkpoint hashes and original failed evaluations</a>. The retained <code>evaluation_sources/</code> files identify the exact evaluator revision.</p>''')
    if (root/'prior_additional/rhac_source/provenance.json').exists():
        section('Relation to RHAC history conditioning','''<p>The RHAC source clarifies which parts of that architecture were transferred into this experiment. The Hadamard encoder is in <code>rhac.py</code>; the current <code>postdown.py</code> implements a separate signed-bucket residual conditioner. The language-model harness reuses the permutation, block-Hadamard and sign construction, then folds a full view into each rank-8 chart so every FFN input reaches every chart coordinate. RHAC's spread views retain their full width before routing.</p>'''+table(['Aspect','RHAC spread-conditioned graft','Demuxed-history KS2 harness'],[
            ['Role of KS2','Correction to intact pretrained SwiGLU activations; base projections stay frozen','Replacement FFN learned from scratch; fixed input/output codes with learned output gains'],
            ['History','Detached completed decoder states, collapsed to an RMS-matched depth mean','Differentiable concatenation of separate post-attention and post-FFN main states plus the side residual'],
            ['Learned routing','Pretrained gate/up/down projections plus learned history landing vectors','Fixed folded Hadamard input and transpose output directions; scalar output gains'],
            ['Local coordinates','sigmoid(g/1.5), original KS2 equation, normally two geometry iterations','Raw projected coordinates, stabilized KS2 equation, one iteration in the baseline'],
            ['Correction placement','Added to SiLU(g), then multiplied by the intact up branch','KS2 output demuxed into the residual stream'],
        ])+'''<p>These differences explain why a successful compressed history conditioner does not by itself establish that fixed multiplexers can replace every learned FFN projection at the same quality. That is an inference about the architectural comparison, not a negative result for RHAC. The current study directly tests learned routing, geometry iteration, coordinate statistics and local equation choices; it does not reproduce the full pretrained RHAC adaptation protocol or infer its quality from source code.</p><p><a href="prior_additional/rhac_source/provenance.json">Pinned local source provenance</a> · <a href="prior_additional/rhac_source/rhac.py">Spread encoder, local correction and history path</a> · <a href="prior_additional/rhac_source/postdown.py">Post-down conditioner</a> · <a href="prior_additional/rhac_source/ks2.py">Original local geometry</a>.</p>''')
    return summary
